---
import SectionLayout from '../layouts/SectionLayout.astro';
import PlainEnglish from '../components/PlainEnglish.astro';
import StatBlock from '../components/StatBlock.astro';
import PowerComparisonDiagram from '../components/diagrams/PowerComparisonDiagram.astro';
import { withBase } from '../utils/url';

const rackStats = [
  { value: '5–10 kW', label: 'Traditional server rack' },
  { value: '50–100 kW', label: 'AI server rack' },
  { value: '100–750 MW', label: 'A single AI data center site' },
  { value: '5+ years', label: 'Typical grid interconnection wait' },
];
---

<SectionLayout
  slug="power-energy"
  kicker="Section 04"
  title="Power & energy"
  dek="Why electricity, not chips, is now the binding constraint on AI buildout."
>
  <PlainEnglish>
    <p>
      Building an AI data center used to be mostly about getting enough GPUs. Now the harder
      problem is getting enough electricity. Power grids can't add capacity fast enough, so tech
      companies are building their own gas plants, restarting old nuclear reactors, and signing
      decades-long power contracts just to keep their computers running.
    </p>
  </PlainEnglish>

  <StatBlock stats={rackStats} />

  <h2>Why power became the bottleneck, not GPUs</h2>
  <p>
    An AI server rack now draws 50-100 kW, versus 5-10 kW for a traditional one, and a single AI data center site
    can require 100-750 MW — enough to power a small city. Demand is growing faster than grids can respond:
    connecting a new power source to the grid now takes 5+ years in many U.S. regions because of an
    interconnection queue backlog, with over 2,300 GW of generation and storage projects currently waiting in
    line. That mismatch — GPUs can be delivered in months, new power in years — is why "we have the chips but
    nowhere to plug them in" has become a common story in this industry.
  </p>

  <h2>The three ways companies are solving it</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Speed to deploy</th><th>Tradeoff</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>On-site natural gas turbines</td>
          <td>Fastest — 12-18 months</td>
          <td>Reliable and weather-independent, but exposes the operator to fuel-price swings and creates a direct carbon liability</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Nuclear <span class="section-note">(restarts + new small modular reactors)</span></td>
          <td>Slow — years, but delivers steady 24/7 power once online</td>
          <td>Microsoft's deal to restart the Three Mile Island plant (now renamed Crane) for roughly 835 MW is the clearest example; Meta has signed a 20-year deal for over 2,600 MW from existing nuclear plants</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Renewables + storage</td>
          <td>Fast to build, but intermittent</td>
          <td>Solar and wind are the largest source of <em>new</em> grid capacity overall, but need batteries or backup power to cover gaps</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <PowerComparisonDiagram />

  <p>
    In practice, most large operators are doing all three at once — natural gas as a fast bridge, renewables for
    volume, and nuclear for reliable long-term baseload — while also signing direct power-purchase agreements
    (PPAs) that lock in electricity from a specific plant for 10-20+ years, bypassing the public grid's queue
    entirely.
  </p>

  <h2>The knock-on effects worth including</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rising electricity bills for ordinary consumers</strong> — in regions with heavy data-center buildout (e.g. parts of Virginia), local electricity prices have risen sharply as grids strain to serve new demand alongside existing customers; regional wholesale power-capacity prices in one major U.S. market rose over 800% between two recent delivery years.</li>
    <li><strong>A political response</strong> — in March 2026, seven major AI/tech companies signed a "Ratepayer Protection Pledge," committing to directly fund grid upgrades themselves rather than passing those costs to residential customers.</li>
    <li><strong>Global electricity demand from data centers is genuinely enormous</strong> — global data-center electricity consumption is on track to roughly double from 2024 to 2026, and AI is the primary driver of that growth.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout-note">
    Securing power increasingly requires the same kind of long-term capital commitments as GPUs do — the two
    bottlenecks (compute and power) end up financed in similar ways. More on that in
    <a href={withBase('/financing')}>Section 06: Financing</a>.
  </div>
</SectionLayout>

<style>
  .section-note {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75em;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    text-transform: none;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
</style>
